## Polished LaTeX

```latex
%=============================================================================
\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}
%=============================================================================

We present \ours{}, which embeds geometric safety awareness into a flow-matching VLA at training time with zero inference overhead. An auxiliary SDF head predicts signed distances from the model's internal representations, and a noise-aware collision-shaping loss penalizes near-collision trajectories. After training, the head is discarded and the base $\pi_{0.5}$ policy is deployed unchanged (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}).


\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_overview.pdf}
\caption{\ours{} overview. \textbf{Left:} During training, an SDF auxiliary head predicts signed distances from the action expert's internal features $f_t$. The collision-shaping loss, gated by noise-aware weight $w(t)$, penalizes trajectories violating safety margin $\delta$. Gradients from both $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SDF}}$ and $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{col}}$ flow back through the shared features into the action expert weights. \textbf{Right:} At inference, the SDF head is discarded; the model runs through the standard \pimodel{} pipeline with zero overhead.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:formulation}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\paragraph{Flow-matching action generation.} We use $t \in [0,1]$ exclusively for the flow-matching noise level and reserve index $j$ for the position within the action chunk of horizon $H$. $\pi_{0.5}$ generates robot action trajectories through conditional flow matching. Given an observation $\mathbf{o}$ (RGB images and a language instruction), the model learns a velocity field $v_\theta(x_t, \mathbf{o}, t)$ that transports samples from a noise distribution to the data distribution of action chunks. Let $a \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times D_a}$ denote a ground-truth action chunk of horizon $H$ and dimension $D_a$, and let $\epsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I)$ be Gaussian noise. The noisy interpolant at time $t \in [0, 1]$ is
\begin{equation}
x_t = (1 - t) \, a + t \, \epsilon,
\end{equation}
where $t = 0$ corresponds to the clean action and $t = 1$ to pure noise. The conditional velocity field target is $u_t = \epsilon - a$, and the flow-matching objective minimizes
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FM}} = \mathbb{E}_{t, a, \epsilon} \left[ \left\| v_\theta(x_t, \mathbf{o}, t) - (\epsilon - a) \right\|^2 \right].
\end{equation}
At inference, actions are generated by integrating the learned velocity field from $t = 1$ (noise) to $t = 0$ (data) using an ODE solver.

\paragraph{Safety as collision avoidance.} We formalize safety through the signed distance field (SDF) between the robot's end-effector and scene obstacles. For a robot configuration $q$ and obstacle set $\mathcal{O}$, the minimum signed distance $s(q) = \min_{b \in \mathcal{O}} d(q, b)$ is positive when the end-effector clears all obstacles and negative upon penetration. A trajectory is safe if $s(q_j) \geq \delta$ for all chunk positions $j \in \{1, \ldots, H\}$, where $\delta > 0$ is a safety margin. Our goal is to train a policy that generates trajectories satisfying this constraint without runtime collision checking.

%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{SDF Auxiliary Head}
\label{sec:sdf_head}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\paragraph{Architecture.} The SDF head is a two-layer MLP that maps the action expert's internal features to per-position signed distance predictions. In $\pi_{0.5}$, the action expert is a Gemma-300M transformer that processes noisy action tokens alongside vision-language context. Let $f_t \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times H \times D}$ denote the action expert's pre-projection features (with $D = 1024$ for the Gemma-300M variant), which are normally projected to action-space dimension $D_a = 32$ by a linear output layer. We tap these features via a forward hook on the output projection layer, requiring no modification to the base model's source code. The SDF head computes
\begin{equation}
\hat{s} = W_2 \, \mathrm{ReLU}(W_1 \, f_t + b_1) + b_2,
\end{equation}
where $W_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{256 \times 1024}$, $W_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times 256}$, and $\hat{s} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times H}$ gives the predicted minimum signed distance for each position in the action chunk. The head is applied independently to each of the $H$ positions and contains approximately 263K parameters (0.09\% of the 300M-parameter action expert).

\paragraph{Ground-truth SDF labels.} Per-position signed distances are precomputed offline using MuJoCo's \texttt{mj\_geomDistance}, measuring minimum distance between end-effector geometries and scene obstacles (excluding grasped objects). Labels are stored as compressed arrays and aligned to action chunks at training time. Details are in Appendix~\ref{app:sdf_labels}.

%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Noise-Aware Collision-Shaping Loss}
\label{sec:loss}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

In the flow-matching framework, high-noise samples ($t \to 1$) carry no geometric structure, so a uniform collision penalty across all noise levels would inject meaningless gradients. We therefore gate the collision penalty with a noise-aware weight that activates only in the low-noise regime.

\paragraph{Noise-aware weight.} We define a time-dependent weight
\begin{equation}
w(t) = \sigma\!\left(\alpha \, (t_0 - t)\right),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma$ is the sigmoid function, $t_0 = 0.5$ is the transition center, and $\alpha = 10$ controls transition sharpness. When $t \ll t_0$ (low noise), $w(t) \approx 1$ and the collision penalty is fully active; when $t \gg t_0$ (high noise), $w(t) \approx 0$ and the penalty is suppressed (Figure~\ref{fig:weight_curve}).

\paragraph{SDF regression loss.} The regression loss trains the SDF head to predict accurate signed distances:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SDF}} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|} \sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{V}} \left(\hat{s}_{i,j} - s_{i,j}\right)^2,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{V}$ is the set of valid sample-position pairs with available ground-truth labels, $\hat{s}_{i,j}$ is the predicted signed distance, and $s_{i,j}$ is the ground truth. Accurate SDF predictions are prerequisite for the collision penalty to produce meaningful gradients.

\paragraph{Collision penalty.} The collision loss penalizes predictions below the safety margin $\delta$:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{col}} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|} \sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{V}} w(t_i) \cdot \mathrm{ReLU}\!\left(\delta - \hat{s}_{i,j}\right),
\end{equation}
where $\delta = 0.02\,\mathrm{m}$ (2\,cm safety margin) and $t_i$ is the flow-matching noise level for sample $i$. The hinge structure ensures zero penalty when $\hat{s}_{i,j} > \delta$ and linear penalty proportional to violation depth otherwise. The noise-aware weight $w(t_i)$ restricts this shaping signal to the low-noise regime where the model's features encode meaningful spatial information.

\paragraph{Total training objective.} The complete loss is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:total_loss}
\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FM}} + \lambda_{\mathrm{SDF}} \cdot \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SDF}} + \lambda_{\mathrm{col}} \cdot \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{col}},
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_{\mathrm{SDF}} = 0.1$ and $\lambda_{\mathrm{col}} = 0.01$. The small collision weight reflects its role as a shaping signal: the flow-matching loss remains the primary objective, and the collision penalty steers the velocity field toward safer regions without dominating optimization. Despite the small $\lambda_{\mathrm{col}}$, the directional gradient signal accumulates across training steps to measurably shift the velocity field (Section~\ref{sec:main_results}).


\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_weight_curve.pdf}
\caption{Noise-aware weight $w(t) = \sigma(\alpha(t_0 - t))$ for $\alpha \in \{5, 10, 20\}$ with $t_0 = 0.5$. The weight suppresses collision penalties at high noise levels ($t > t_0$), where action predictions are noise-dominated and SDF estimates are unreliable, and activates them in the low-noise regime ($t < t_0$) where predictions carry spatial structure. $t_0 = 0.5$ marks the noise-to-signal transition; $\alpha$ controls sharpness.}
\label{fig:weight_curve}
\end{figure}

%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Training Pipeline}
\label{sec:training}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\ours{} follows the standard $\pi_{0.5}$ fine-tuning pipeline with full parameter updates (not LoRA), AdamW optimizer, and DDP across two GPUs. Complete hyperparameters are in Appendix~\ref{app:training}. Gradients from both $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SDF}}$ and $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{col}}$ propagate through the shared features into the action expert weights (i.e., the SDF head's input features are not detached from the computation graph). This end-to-end gradient flow is the mechanism by which collision awareness becomes encoded in the velocity field. SDF labels cover ${\sim}$75\% of training tasks; samples without labels contribute only $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FM}}$.

%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Inference: Zero-Overhead Deployment}
\label{sec:inference}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

At inference, the SDF head is discarded. The model runs through the unmodified $\pi_{0.5}$ pipeline with the same checkpoint format, the same ODE solver, and the same RGB-only inputs. No SDF queries, depth maps, or collision solvers are needed at deployment. Safety is encoded in the modified velocity field: the collision-shaping gradients during training have steered action generation toward obstacle-avoiding trajectories.

This design is analogous to guidance distillation \citep{meng2023distillation}: the SDF head serves as a ``geometric safety classifier'' whose gradients are internalized by the model, after which the classifier is discarded. A detailed comparison is in Appendix~\ref{app:distillation}.
